## BasicBlock.source_block
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A basic block is a list of instructions with one way in and one way out (no jumps in the middle).
; Binja has blocks at many levels: assembly, LLIL, MLIL, HLIL. Each IL level has its own blocks.
;
; source_block gives you the ASSEMBLY block that a block came from. It is read-only.
;   - On an assembly block (like `for bb in func`), it gives back the same block.
;     So in that loop it is not useful.
;   - On an IL block (LLIL / MLIL / HLIL), it gives the assembly block it was made from.
;     This is the useful case.
;
; Why you need it: IL blocks use instruction INDEXES for start/end, not addresses.
; source_block lets you go back to real addresses in the binary.

bb.source_block                     # the assembly block this block came from (None if none)  @binja @api @basicblock  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/basicblock.py:363>

; Example: for every HLIL block, print its index range and the real address range
;
;     for func in bv.functions:
;         for hb in func.hlil.basic_blocks:
;             src = hb.source_block
;             print(hb.start, hb.end, "->", hex(src.start), hex(src.end))
;
; Note: hb.start and hb.end are indexes (0, 1, 2 ...). src.start and src.end are addresses.
; Note: after optimization, one IL block can come from many asm blocks.
;       source_block gives only one of them.


## callingconvention module
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A calling convention is a set of rules for a function call:
;   - Where do the arguments go? (which registers, or the stack)
;   - Where does the return value go?
;   - Who cleans the stack after the call: the caller or the called function?
;   - Which registers can the called function destroy, and which must it keep safe?
;
; Examples: cdecl, stdcall, fastcall (x86), sysv (Linux x86_64), win64 (Windows x86_64).
;
; Binja must know the calling convention to show correct arguments in HLIL.
; If the convention is wrong, the arguments in the decompiler are wrong too.

; --- Where to get a convention
cc = current_function.calling_convention
func.calling_convention                  # the convention of one function. You can also set it  @binja @api @cc  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/function.py:1428>
bv.platform.calling_conventions          # list of all conventions for this platform             @binja @api @cc  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/platform.py:479>
bv.platform.default_calling_convention   # the normal convention for this platform               @binja @api @cc  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/platform.py:393>
bv.arch.calling_conventions              # dict: name -> convention, for this CPU                @binja @api @cc  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/architecture.py:1109>

; --- What is inside a convention (cc)
cc = current_function.calling_convention
cc.name                                  # name, like "sysv"                                     @binja @api @cc  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/callingconvention.py:88>
cc.int_arg_regs                          # registers for integer and pointer arguments, in order @binja @api @cc
cc.float_arg_regs                        # registers for float arguments, in order               @binja @api @cc
cc.int_return_reg                        # register for the integer return value                 @binja @api @cc
cc.float_return_reg                      # register for the float return value                   @binja @api @cc
cc.caller_saved_regs                     # registers the called function can destroy             @binja @api @cc
cc.callee_saved_regs                     # registers the called function must keep safe          @binja @api @cc
cc.stack_adjusted_on_return              # True = called function cleans the stack (like stdcall) @binja @api @cc


; --- Example 1: see the convention of the current function
;
;     cc = current_function.calling_convention
;     print(cc)                       # sysv
;     print(repr(cc))                 # <calling convention: x86_64 sysv>
;     print("args in: ", cc.int_arg_regs)
;     print("return in:", cc.int_return_reg)
;
; On Linux x86_64 you will see rdi, rsi, rdx, rcx, r8, r9 for the arguments and rax for the return value.


; --- Example 2: list all conventions you can use
;
;     print("default:", bv.platform.default_calling_convention)
;     for cc in bv.platform.calling_conventions:
;         print(cc.name)


; --- Example 3: which register holds argument number N?
;
;     cc = current_function.calling_convention
;     n = 2                            # third argument (we count from 0)
;     print(cc.int_arg_regs[n])        # rdx on sysv
;
; This is useful when you read assembly: before a `call`, look at this register to see the argument.


; --- Example 4: count functions by convention
;
;     from collections import Counter
;     c = Counter(str(f.calling_convention) for f in bv.functions)
;     print(c)                         # Counter({'sysv': 120, 'None': 2})
;
; If a function has a different convention from the rest, look at it.
; It can be hand-written assembly or obfuscated code.


; --- Example 5: change the convention of a function
; Use this when Binja guessed wrong and the arguments in HLIL look bad.
;
;     cc = bv.arch.calling_conventions["fastcall"]   # x86 32-bit example
;     current_function.calling_convention = cc
;
; After this, Binja analyzes the function again and the HLIL changes.


; --- Example 6: compare two conventions
;
;     for name, cc in bv.arch.calling_conventions.items():
;         print(name, "| args:", cc.int_arg_regs, "| cleans stack:", cc.stack_adjusted_on_return)
;
; On x86 (32-bit) you will see that stdcall cleans the stack and cdecl does not.


; --- Example 7: make your own convention (advanced)
; Use this for strange code, for example a function that gets its arguments in eax and ebx.
; You write a class with the rules, then register it.
;
;     from binaryninja import CallingConvention
;
;     class MyConv(CallingConvention):
;         name = "my_eax_ebx"
;         int_arg_regs = ["eax", "ebx"]
;         int_return_reg = "eax"
;         caller_saved_regs = ["eax", "ecx", "edx"]
;         callee_saved_regs = ["ebx", "esi", "edi", "ebp"]
;
;     cc = MyConv(bv.arch, "my_eax_ebx")
;     bv.platform.register_calling_convention(cc)
;     current_function.calling_convention = cc


; --- Notes
; - current_function exists only in the Binja GUI Python console. In a script, use a function from bv.functions.
; - func.calling_convention can be None if Binja does not know it.
; - bv.arch.calling_conventions is a dict (use a name). bv.platform.calling_conventions is a list.








## commonil module
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Binja has 3 IL levels: LLIL, MLIL, HLIL. Each level has its own instruction classes:
;     LowLevelILCall, MediumLevelILCall, HighLevelILCall ...
; This is a problem when you want to ask a simple question like "is this a call?".
; You must check 3 different classes.
;
; commonil solves this. It has small "label" classes that are shared by all 3 levels.
; Every IL instruction class also inherits one or more of these labels. Example from the source:
;     class HighLevelILCall(HighLevelILInstruction, Localcall)
;     class MediumLevelILCall(MediumLevelILCallBase, Localcall)
;     class LowLevelILCall(LowLevelILInstruction, Localcall)
; So isinstance(inst, Localcall) works on LLIL, MLIL and HLIL.
;
; The label classes are empty (only "pass"). They have no data. They only tell you the KIND of instruction.

from binaryninja.commonil import *    # import all the label classes  @binja @api @il  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/commonil.py>

; --- The label classes (a child is also its parent: Tailcall is a Localcall, and also a Call)
;
;   Constant              a number or address: HLIL_CONST, HLIL_CONST_PTR, HLIL_FLOAT_CONST ...
;   BinaryOperation       2 inputs (a + b)
;     Comparison          a == b, a < b ...
;   UnaryOperation        1 input (-a, ~a)
;   Arithmetic            math
;     Carry               math with carry flag (adc, sbb)
;     DoublePrecision     math with a double-size result
;   Signed                signed version of an operation
;   FloatingPoint         float operation
;   ControlFlow           changes where the code goes (if, jump, goto ...)
;     Terminal            ends the path
;       Return            return
;     Loop                while, do-while, for (HLIL)
;     Call                any call
;       Syscall           system call
;       Localcall         normal call to a function
;         Tailcall        jump to a function at the end (call + return together)
;   Memory, Load, Store   memory read / write
;   SetVar                writes to a variable
;   SetReg                writes to a register
;   StackOperation        push / pop
;   RegisterStack         x87 float register stack
;   Intrinsic             special CPU instruction with no simple IL (like cpuid, rdtsc)
;   VariableInstruction   uses a variable
;     SSAVariableInstruction      SSA version
;     AliasedVariableInstruction  variable that memory can also change
;   SSA
;     Phi                 SSA phi node (picks a value from one of the paths)


; --- Example 1: find all calls in a function, at any IL level
;
from binaryninja.commonil import Call
f = bv.get_functions_by_name("main")[0]     # pick a function by name
for il in [f.llil, f.mlil, f.hlil]:
    for inst in il.instructions:
        if isinstance(inst, Call):
            print(hex(inst.address), inst)
;
; Note: in HLIL a call is often INSIDE another instruction (x = foo()).
; il.instructions gives only the top level. Use traverse (Example 3) to find all of them.


; --- Example 2: Call vs Localcall vs Syscall
;
from binaryninja.commonil import Syscall, Localcall
f = bv.get_functions_by_name("main")[0]     # pick a function by name
for inst in f.mlil.instructions:
    if isinstance(inst, Syscall):
        print("syscall  ", hex(inst.address), inst)
    elif isinstance(inst, Localcall):
        print("call     ", hex(inst.address), inst)


; --- Example 3: traverse + labels -> find all constants in HLIL
; traverse calls your function on every instruction and every sub-instruction.
;
from binaryninja.commonil import Constant
f = bv.get_functions_by_name("main")[0]     # pick a function by name
def get_const(inst):
    if isinstance(inst, Constant):
        return inst.constant

for c in f.hlil.traverse(get_const):
    if c is not None:
        print(hex(c))
;
; HLIL traverse source: <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/highlevelil.py:2858>


; --- Example 6: see the label tree of a class (GUI only)
;
from binaryninja.highlevelil import HighLevelILCall
HighLevelILCall.show_hierarchy_graph()
;
; It opens a graph that shows all parents: HighLevelILCall -> Localcall -> Call -> ControlFlow -> BaseILInstruction.


; --- ILSourceLocation (also in commonil)
; It remembers where an IL expression came from: the address and the operand number.
; Binja uses it when it lifts code, so HLIL can show the right address and number format.

inst.source_location                               # ILSourceLocation of an instruction       @binja @api @il  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/highlevelil.py:496>
ILSourceLocation.from_instruction(inst)            # same thing, works for LLIL/MLIL/HLIL     @binja @api @il  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/commonil.py:253>
loc.address, loc.source_operand                    # the address and the operand number       @binja @api @il

; Example: print where an HLIL instruction came from
;
;     f = bv.get_functions_by_name("main")[0]     # pick a function by name
;     for inst in f.hlil.instructions:
;         print(inst.source_location)      # <ILSourceLocation: 401136, 0 (from HLIL ...)>
;
; You need it mostly when you WRITE IL yourself (a lifter or a workflow).
; Many IL builder functions take loc=..., for example: il.nop(loc=...)
; So the new expression keeps the address of the old one.












## component module
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A Component is like a FOLDER for your analysis.
; You put functions and data variables in it, to keep your work in order.
; Example: one folder "crypto" for all the crypto functions, one folder "network" for the network functions.
;
; - A component can hold: functions, data variables, and other components (sub-folders).
; - One function can be in MORE than one component.
; - Putting a function in a component does not change the code. It is only for you (organizing).
; - There is always one top folder: the root component. You can not remove it.
; - Every component has a guid (a unique id). The guid stays the same after you save and open the database again.
;
; You do NOT create a Component with Component(...). You use bv.create_component(...).

; --- On the BinaryView (bv)
bv.create_component(name, parent)          # make a new component. parent is optional (Component or guid)   @binja @api @component  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/binaryview.py:8112>
bv.root_component                          # the top component. All components are under it                  @binja @api @component  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/binaryview.py:8102>
bv.get_component_by_path("/A/B")           # find a component by its path (case-sensitive)                   @binja @api @component  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/binaryview.py:8069>
bv.get_component(guid)                     # find a component by its guid (fast, best way)                   @binja @api @component  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/binaryview.py:8057>
bv.remove_component(c)                     # delete a component from the tree                                @binja @api @component  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/binaryview.py:8143>

; --- On a Component (c)
c.add_function(func)                       # put a function in it                                            @binja @api @component  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/component.py:70>
c.remove_function(func)                    # take a function out of it                                       @binja @api @component
c.contains_function(func)                  # True if the function is in it                                   @binja @api @component
c.add_component(other)                     # put another component inside it (move)                          @binja @api @component
c.remove_component(other)                  # take a sub-component out. It MOVES to root, it is not deleted   @binja @api @component  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/component.py:115>
c.add_data_variable(dv)                    # put a data variable in it                                       @binja @api @component
c.name                                     # name. You can set it (c.name = "new")                           @binja @api @component
c.display_name                             # name shown in the GUI, like "crypto (1)" if two have the same name  @binja @api @component
c.guid                                     # unique id (string)                                              @binja @api @component
c.parent                                   # the parent component                                            @binja @api @component
c.components                               # list of sub-components                                          @binja @api @component
c.functions                                # functions in it                                                 @binja @api @component
c.data_variables                           # data variables in it                                            @binja @api @component
c.get_referenced_types(recursive=False)    # types used by the functions in it                               @binja @api @component  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/component.py:314>
c.get_referenced_data_variables(recursive=False)  # data variables used by the functions in it               @binja @api @component  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/component.py:292>

; --- On a Function / DataVariable
func.components                            # list of components that hold this function                     @binja @api @component  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/function.py:1758>
dv.components                              # list of components that hold this data variable                @binja @api @component

; All examples below run in the Binja GUI Python console.
; bv = the binary you have open (the console gives you this variable).


; --- Example 1: make a component and put one function in it
;
;     c = bv.create_component(name="crypto")
;     f = bv.get_functions_by_name("main")[0]          # pick a function by name
;     c.add_function(f)
;     print(c.contains_function(f))                    # True
;     print(list(c.functions))


; --- Example 2: sub-components and find by path
;
;     net  = bv.create_component(name="network")
;     send = bv.create_component(name="send", parent=net)     # "send" is inside "network"
;     found = bv.get_component_by_path("/network/send")
;     print(found == send)                             # True
;     print(send.parent)                               # the "network" component


; --- Example 3: group all functions by a name prefix
; Put every function whose name starts with "SSL_" into one component.
;
;     c = bv.create_component(name="openssl")
;     for f in bv.functions:
;         if f.name.startswith("SSL_"):
;             c.add_function(f)
;     print(len(list(c.functions)), "functions added")


; --- Example 4: group functions that call a dangerous function
; Every function that calls strcpy goes into "uses_strcpy". Good for bug hunting.
;
;     c = bv.create_component(name="uses_strcpy")
;     for target in bv.get_functions_by_name("strcpy"):
;         for caller in target.callers:
;             c.add_function(caller)
;     print(list(c.functions))


; --- Example 5: in which components is a function?
;
;     f = bv.get_functions_by_name("main")[0]          # pick a function by name
;     for c in f.components:
;         print(c.display_name)


; --- Example 6: print the whole tree
; print(component) shows the component, its functions, and its sub-components as a tree.
;
;     print(bv.root_component)


; --- Example 7: put a data variable in a component
;
;     c = bv.create_component(name="globals")
;     for addr, dv in bv.data_vars.items():
;         if dv.name and dv.name.startswith("g_"):
;             c.add_data_variable(dv)
;     print(list(c.data_variables))


; --- Example 8: save the guid, get the component back later
; A Python variable is lost when you close Binja. The guid is not.
;
;     c = bv.create_component(name="todo")
;     guid = c.guid
;     print(guid)                                      # copy this string somewhere
;     # ... later, maybe in another session:
;     c2 = bv.get_component(guid)
;     print(c2.name)                                   # todo


; --- Example 9: what types and globals does a component use?
;
;     c = bv.create_component(name="main_only")
;     c.add_function(bv.get_functions_by_name("main")[0])
;     for t in c.get_referenced_types():
;         print("type:", t)
;     for dv in c.get_referenced_data_variables():
;         print("data var:", hex(dv.address), dv.type)


; --- Example 10: rename, move out, delete
;
;     a = bv.create_component(name="old_name")
;     b = bv.create_component(name="child", parent=a)
;     a.name = "new_name"                              # rename
;     a.remove_component(b)                            # b moves to root. It still exists
;     bv.remove_component(b)                           # now b is really deleted
;     bv.remove_component(a)


; --- Notes
; - c.remove_component(x) MOVES x to root. bv.remove_component(x) DELETES x.
; - Use bv.get_component(guid) in scripts. get_component_by_path uses display_name and is slower.
; - Two components with the same name under the same parent: the second one gets display_name "name (1)".
; - You can react to changes (component added, moved, function added ...) with BinaryDataNotification:
;   component_added, component_moved, component_function_added ...  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/binaryview.py:431>
; - I did not run these examples.











## constantrenderer module (ConstantRenderer)
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A ConstantRenderer changes HOW a number is SHOWN in HLIL (the decompiler view).
; It does not change the code or the value. It only changes the text you see.
;
; Example: HLIL shows   if (x == 0x67452301)
;          with your renderer it can show   if (x == MD5_INIT_A)
;
; How it works:
;   1. You write a class that inherits ConstantRenderer.
;   2. You give it a unique renderer_name.
;   3. Binja calls your methods for every constant it draws in HLIL.
;   4. If you want to change the text: add tokens and return True.
;      If not: return False, and Binja shows the number in the normal way.
;
; The methods you can write (all are optional):
;   is_valid_for_type(func, type)                         -> True/False. A fast filter: "do I care about this type?"
;   render_constant(instr, type, val, tokens, settings, precedence)
;                                                         -> for normal numbers (not pointers)
;   render_constant_pointer(instr, type, val, tokens, settings, symbol_display, precedence)
;                                                         -> for pointer numbers (addresses)
;
; The arguments:
;   instr   -> the HLIL expression of the constant
;   type    -> the type of the constant (IntegerType, PointerType ...)
;   val     -> the number (int)
;   tokens  -> where you write your text: tokens.append(InstructionTextToken(...))
;
; WARNING (from the source): Python renderers can be SLOW. They run for every constant on screen.
; Always write is_valid_for_type, so Binja skips the types you do not need.

ConstantRenderer                          # base class you inherit from                              @binja @api @render  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/constantrenderer.py>
MyRenderer().register()                   # turn your renderer on (run it one time)                  @binja @api @render
renderer_name = "my_name"                 # class variable. Must be set, or register() fails         @binja @api @render
tokens.append(InstructionTextToken(t, s)) # write text s, with token type t                         @binja @api @render  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/languagerepresentation.py:41>
list(ConstantRenderer)                    # all registered renderers                                @binja @api @render
ConstantRenderer["name"]                  # get one renderer by name (KeyError if not found)        @binja @api @render
"name" in ConstantRenderer                # True if a renderer with this name exists                @binja @api @render
/opt/binaryninja/examples/python/bid64_constant.py   # official example: shows BID64 decimal floats      @binja @example @render  <file:/opt/binaryninja/examples/python/bid64_constant.py>

; Token types you will use most (InstructionTextTokenType):
;   IntegerToken             a number
;   FloatingPointToken       a float number
;   EnumerationMemberToken   a name of a known value (looks like an enum)
;   TextToken                normal text
;   StringToken              a string


; --- Example 1: list the renderers that exist now
;
;     from binaryninja import ConstantRenderer
;     for r in ConstantRenderer:
;         print(r.name)


; --- Example 2: show known magic numbers as names
; Crypto code uses fixed numbers. Show their names, so you see "this is MD5" fast.
;
;     from binaryninja import (ConstantRenderer, InstructionTextToken,
;                              InstructionTextTokenType, IntegerType)
;
;     MAGIC = {
;         0x67452301: "MD5_SHA1_INIT_A",
;         0xefcdab89: "MD5_SHA1_INIT_B",
;         0x98badcfe: "MD5_SHA1_INIT_C",
;         0x10325476: "MD5_SHA1_INIT_D",
;         0x9e3779b9: "TEA_DELTA",
;         0xedb88320: "CRC32_POLY",
;     }
;
;     class MagicRenderer(ConstantRenderer):
;         renderer_name = "magic_numbers"
;
;         def is_valid_for_type(self, func, type):
;             return isinstance(type, IntegerType)          # only integers, skip the rest (faster)
;
;         def render_constant(self, instr, type, val, tokens, settings, precedence):
;             val &= 0xffffffff                              # the number can be negative; make it 32-bit
;             if val not in MAGIC:
;                 return False                               # not ours -> Binja shows the normal number
;             tokens.append(InstructionTextToken(InstructionTextTokenType.EnumerationMemberToken, MAGIC[val]))
;             return True
;
;     MagicRenderer().register()
;
; Before:  int32_t a = 0x67452301;
; After:   int32_t a = MD5_SHA1_INIT_A;


; --- Example 3: show 4-byte numbers that are ASCII text as 'ABCD'
; Many file formats and malware use 4 letters as a tag: 'RIFF', 'PK\x03\x04', 'MZ' ...
; HLIL shows 0x46464952. This renderer shows 'RIFF' (little endian, like x86 memory).
;
;     from binaryninja import (ConstantRenderer, InstructionTextToken,
;                              InstructionTextTokenType, IntegerType)
;
;     class FourCCRenderer(ConstantRenderer):
;         renderer_name = "fourcc"
;
;         def is_valid_for_type(self, func, type):
;             return isinstance(type, IntegerType) and type.width == 4
;
;         def render_constant(self, instr, type, val, tokens, settings, precedence):
;             b = (val & 0xffffffff).to_bytes(4, "little")
;             if not all(0x20 <= c < 0x7f for c in b):      # all 4 bytes must be printable letters
;                 return False
;             tokens.append(InstructionTextToken(InstructionTextTokenType.StringToken, "'" + b.decode() + "'"))
;             return True
;
;     FourCCRenderer().register()
;
; Before:  if (magic == 0x46464952)
; After:   if (magic == 'RIFF')


; --- Example 4: show pointers as section + offset
; render_constant_pointer is called for addresses. Here: show 0x4010 as  .data+0x10
;
;     from binaryninja import (ConstantRenderer, InstructionTextToken,
;                              InstructionTextTokenType, PointerType)
;
;     class SectionPtrRenderer(ConstantRenderer):
;         renderer_name = "section_ptr"
;
;         def is_valid_for_type(self, func, type):
;             return isinstance(type, PointerType)
;
;         def render_constant_pointer(self, instr, type, val, tokens, settings, symbol_display, precedence):
;             bv = instr.function.view                       # the BinaryView of this function
;             if bv.get_symbol_at(val) is not None:
;                 return False                               # it has a name already -> keep the normal view
;             sections = bv.get_sections_at(val)
;             if not sections:
;                 return False
;             s = sections[0]
;             tokens.append(InstructionTextToken(InstructionTextTokenType.TextToken, f"{s.name}+{val - s.start:#x}"))
;             return True
;
;     SectionPtrRenderer().register()


; --- Example 5: check that your renderer is on
;
;     from binaryninja import ConstantRenderer
;     print("magic_numbers" in ConstantRenderer)            # True after Example 2
;     print(ConstantRenderer["magic_numbers"].name)


; --- Notes
; - Run register() ONE time. Running the script again registers it again.
;   To make it load every time, put the file in your Binja plugins folder.
; - The value can come as a negative number (signed). Use val & 0xffffffff (or the right size) before you compare.
; - Return False when it is not your case. Then other renderers (or the normal view) can show it.
; - It only changes HLIL text. Your scripts that read inst.constant still get the real number.
; - If the HLIL does not change at once, open the function again.
; - I did not run these examples.










## database module
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; When you save your work in Binja, it makes a .bndb file. The database module lets you look INSIDE this file.
; It is a LOW-LEVEL module. Most of the time you do not need it. Use it when you want to:
;   - see the history of saves (snapshots)
;   - get the ORIGINAL binary back from a .bndb file
;   - store your own small data inside the .bndb
;
; Words:
;   Database       the .bndb file
;   Snapshot       one save. Every time you save, Binja adds a new snapshot.
;                  Snapshots make a tree: each snapshot has parent(s) and children.
;   Auto snapshot  a save that Binja made by itself (auto-save)
;   Global         a key -> value pair stored in the whole database (not in one snapshot)
;   KeyValueStore  a dictionary of raw data inside a snapshot (key -> DataBuffer)
;
; IMPORTANT: bv.file.database is None if the binary is NOT saved as .bndb yet.
; Open a .bndb file, or save your work first (File -> Save).

; --- Get a database
bv.file.database                          # the Database of the open file (None if no .bndb)          @binja @api @db  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/filemetadata.py:310>
Database.open_existing(path)              # open a .bndb file directly                                @binja @api @db  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/database.py:250>
bv.create_database(path)                  # save the current work as a NEW .bndb file                 @binja @api @db  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/binaryview.py:5042>
bv.save_auto_snapshot()                   # save again into the .bndb you already made                @binja @api @db  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/binaryview.py:5066>

; --- Database (db)
db.snapshots                              # list of all snapshots                                     @binja @api @db
db.current_snapshot                       # the snapshot you are using now. You can set it            @binja @api @db
db.get_snapshot(id)  /  db[id]            # one snapshot by its id (None if not found)                @binja @api @db
db.write_global(key, text)                # store a text value in the database                        @binja @api @db
db.read_global(key)                       # read it back                                              @binja @api @db
db.globals                                # dict of all globals                                       @binja @api @db
db.write_global_data(key, DataBuffer)     # store binary data                                         @binja @api @db
db.read_global_data(key)                  # read binary data (DataBuffer)                             @binja @api @db
db.trim_snapshot(id)                      # delete the data of a snapshot, keep it in the tree        @binja @api @db
db.remove_snapshot(id)                    # delete a snapshot fully (error if it has children)        @binja @api @db
db.analysis_cache                         # KeyValueStore of the analysis cache                       @binja @api @db

; --- Snapshot (snap)
snap.id                                   # number                                                    @binja @api @db  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/database.py:121>
snap.name                                 # name. You can set it                                      @binja @api @db
snap.is_auto_save                         # True if Binja saved it by itself                          @binja @api @db
snap.parents / snap.first_parent          # the snapshot(s) before it                                 @binja @api @db
snap.children                             # the snapshot(s) after it                                  @binja @api @db
snap.has_ancestor(other)                  # True if "other" is before it in the tree                  @binja @api @db
snap.has_contents                         # False if the snapshot was trimmed                         @binja @api @db
snap.file_contents                        # the ORIGINAL binary bytes at this save (DataBuffer)       @binja @api @db
snap.file_contents_hash                   # hash of these bytes                                       @binja @api @db
snap.data                                 # KeyValueStore with the saved analysis data                @binja @api @db

; --- KeyValueStore (kvs)
kvs.keys                                  # list of keys                                              @binja @api @db  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/database.py:30>
kvs[key]  /  kvs.get_value(key)           # value as DataBuffer                                       @binja @api @db
kvs[key] = DataBuffer(...)                # set a value                                               @binja @api @db

; All examples below run in the Binja GUI Python console, with a .bndb file open.
; bv = the binary you have open (the console gives you this variable).


; --- Example 1: is my file a database?
;
;     db = bv.file.database
;     if db is None:
;         print("Not a .bndb yet. Save your work first.")
;     else:
;         print("Database with", len(db.snapshots), "snapshots")


; --- Example 2: see the save history
;
;     db = bv.file.database
;     for snap in db.snapshots:
;         parents = [p.id for p in snap.parents]
;         print(snap.id, repr(snap.name), "auto" if snap.is_auto_save else "manual", "parents:", parents)
;     print("current:", db.current_snapshot.id)


; --- Example 3: get the ORIGINAL binary back from a .bndb
; Someone sends you only a .bndb file. You want the real binary to run it or open it in another tool.
;
;     db = bv.file.database
;     snap = db.current_snapshot
;     if snap.has_contents:
;         data = bytes(snap.file_contents)                 # DataBuffer -> Python bytes
;         with open("/tmp/original.bin", "wb") as out:
;             out.write(data)
;         print("wrote", len(data), "bytes")
;
; Note: these are the bytes at the time of the save. If you patched the binary in Binja, the patch is in them.


; --- Example 4: did the binary change between two saves?
;
;     db = bv.file.database
;     snaps = db.snapshots
;     first, last = snaps[0], snaps[-1]
;     if first.has_contents and last.has_contents:
;         same = bytes(first.file_contents_hash) == bytes(last.file_contents_hash)
;         print("same bytes" if same else "the binary was patched")


; --- Example 5: store your own text inside the .bndb
;
;     db = bv.file.database
;     db.write_global("my_notes", "main() decrypts the config at 0x401200")
;     print(db.read_global("my_notes"))
;     print(db.globals)                                    # all keys, also the ones Binja uses
;
; Note: for normal plugin data, bv.store_metadata(key, value) / bv.query_metadata(key) is easier.
;       It also saves in the .bndb. Globals are the low-level way.


; --- Example 6: store binary data
;
;     from binaryninja import DataBuffer
;     db = bv.file.database
;     db.write_global_data("my_blob", DataBuffer(b"\x90\x90\xcc"))
;     print(bytes(db.read_global_data("my_blob")))         # b'\x90\x90\xcc'


; --- Example 7: look inside a snapshot's raw data
;
;     db = bv.file.database
;     kvs = db.current_snapshot.data
;     for key in kvs.keys:
;         print(key, len(kvs[key]))                        # key name and size in bytes


; --- Example 8: open a .bndb without opening it in the GUI
;
;     from binaryninja import Database
;     db = Database.open_existing("/path/to/file.bndb")
;     for snap in db.snapshots:
;         print(snap.id, snap.name)


; --- Example 9: make a .bndb from a script, then save again
;
;     path = bv.file.filename + ".bndb"
;     print(bv.create_database(path))                      # True = saved
;     # ... do more work: rename functions, add comments ...
;     print(bv.save_auto_snapshot())                       # True = saved again, a new snapshot


; --- Notes
; - You will use bv.file.database mostly to READ. Changing snapshots by hand can break your database.
; - Make a COPY of the .bndb before you try trim_snapshot, remove_snapshot, or setting current_snapshot.
; - remove_snapshot fails if the snapshot has children. Remove the children first.
; - snap.file_contents fails (assert) if has_contents is False. Check has_contents first.
; - snap.undo_entries is not available in Python (NotImplementedError).
; - Database.open_existing raises RuntimeError if the file can not be opened.
; - I did not run these examples.














## databuffer module (DataBuffer)
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A DataBuffer is a block of raw bytes that lives in the Binja CORE (C++), not in Python.
; Think of it as Binja's own version of Python "bytes".
;
; Why does it exist? Many Binja APIs talk to the C++ core, and they give or take a DataBuffer:
;   - database:  snap.file_contents, db.read_global_data(...)        (see "database module")
;   - transform: decoders/encoders (base64, xor, zlib ...)
;   - KeyValueStore values
;
; The normal way to work with it:
;   1. Get a DataBuffer from an API (or make one).
;   2. Change it to Python bytes with bytes(buf).
;   3. Work with the bytes in normal Python.
;   4. If an API needs a DataBuffer, make one again: DataBuffer(my_bytes).
;
; GOOD NEWS: DataBuffer works WITHOUT a headless license. You can test it in a normal Python script.
; All outputs in this section are REAL (I ran them with: PYTHONPATH=/opt/binaryninja/python python3).

; --- Make a DataBuffer
DataBuffer(b"\x90\x90")                   # from bytes                                               @binja @api @databuffer  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/databuffer.py:31>
DataBuffer(bytearray(...))                # from bytearray                                           @binja @api @databuffer
DataBuffer("text")                        # from a str (saved as UTF-8)                              @binja @api @databuffer
DataBuffer(16)                            # 16 zero bytes                                            @binja @api @databuffer
DataBuffer(other_buf)                     # a COPY of another DataBuffer                             @binja @api @databuffer

; --- Read it
bytes(buf)                                # all bytes as Python bytes  (the most used!)              @binja @api @databuffer
len(buf)                                  # size in bytes                                            @binja @api @databuffer
buf[0]                                    # one byte, as bytes: b'h' (NOT an int like Python bytes)  @binja @api @databuffer
buf[0:5]                                  # a slice, as bytes                                        @binja @api @databuffer
buf[0, 2:4]                               # many pieces joined together, as bytes                    @binja @api @databuffer
str(buf)                                  # bytes as text (UTF-8, or latin-1 if not valid UTF-8)     @binja @api @databuffer
buf == other                              # True if same bytes                                       @binja @api @databuffer

; --- Helpers (each returns a NEW value)
buf.escape()                              # text with \x00, \n ... for non-printable bytes           @binja @api @databuffer
buf.unescape()                            # opposite of escape -> DataBuffer                         @binja @api @databuffer
buf.base64_encode()                       # -> str                                                   @binja @api @databuffer
buf.base64_decode()                       # -> DataBuffer                                            @binja @api @databuffer
buf.zlib_compress()                       # -> DataBuffer                                            @binja @api @databuffer
buf.zlib_decompress()                     # -> DataBuffer, or None if it is not zlib data            @binja @api @databuffer
escape_string(b"...")                     # same as DataBuffer(b"...").escape()                      @binja @api @databuffer
unescape_string(b"...")                   # same as DataBuffer(b"...").unescape()                    @binja @api @databuffer


; --- Example 1: basics
;
;     from binaryninja import DataBuffer
;     d = DataBuffer(b"hello world")
;     print(len(d))                  # 11
;     print(bytes(d))                # b'hello world'
;     print(d[0], d[-1])             # b'h' b'd'      <- bytes, not numbers
;     print(d[0:5])                  # b'hello'
;     print(bytes(DataBuffer(4)))      # b'\x00\x00\x00\x00'


; --- Example 2: escape -> see hidden bytes as text
; Good for printing data that has zero bytes or newlines.
;
;     from binaryninja import DataBuffer
;     print(DataBuffer(b"a\x00b\n").escape())             # a\x00b\n
;     print(bytes(DataBuffer(b"a\\x41b").unescape()))     # b'aAb'


; --- Example 3: base64
;
;     from binaryninja import DataBuffer
;     print(DataBuffer(b"hello world").base64_encode())           # aGVsbG8gd29ybGQ=
;     print(bytes(DataBuffer("aGVsbG8=").base64_decode()))        # b'hello'


; --- Example 4: zlib
; Malware and firmware often hide data with zlib. You can unpack it fast.
;
;     from binaryninja import DataBuffer
;     z = DataBuffer(b"hello world").zlib_compress()
;     print(len(z))                                       # 19
;     print(bytes(z.zlib_decompress()))                   # b'hello world'
;     print(DataBuffer(b"not zlib").zlib_decompress())    # None  <- check for None!


; --- Example 5: unpack zlib data that is inside the binary
; bv.read gives Python bytes. Put them in a DataBuffer to use zlib_decompress.
;
;     from binaryninja import DataBuffer
;     # bv = the binary you have open in the GUI (the console gives you this variable)
;     addr, size = 0x404000, 0x200                         # change to your data
;     out = DataBuffer(bv.read(addr, size)).zlib_decompress()
;     if out is None:
;         print("not zlib")
;     else:
;         print(DataBuffer(bytes(out)[:64]).escape())     # first 64 bytes, readable


; --- Example 6: CHANGE bytes -> do it in Python, NOT in the DataBuffer
; WARNING: writing into a DataBuffer with [] is BROKEN in this version (I tested it):
;
;     from binaryninja import DataBuffer
;     x = DataBuffer(b"ABCD");  x[0] = b"Z"
;     print(bytes(x))                # b'\x00BCD'    <- WRONG, it wrote 0x00, not 'Z'
;
;     y = DataBuffer(b"ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOP");  y[1:3] = b"XY"
;     print(bytes(y))                # b'A\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00GHIJKLMNOP'  <- WRONG, and 5 bytes broken, not 2
;
; Only a slice with a DIFFERENT length works:
;     y = DataBuffer(b"ABCD");  y[1:3] = b"XYZ"
;     print(bytes(y))                # b'AXYZD'      <- OK
;
; The SAFE way: bytearray -> change -> new DataBuffer
;
;     from binaryninja import DataBuffer
;     buf = DataBuffer(b"ABCD")
;     b = bytearray(bytes(buf))
;     b[0] = ord("Z")
;     buf = DataBuffer(bytes(b))
;     print(bytes(buf))              # b'ZBCD'


; --- Example 7: xor decode (a common RE task)
;
;     from binaryninja import DataBuffer
;     enc = DataBuffer(bytes(c ^ 0x41 for c in b"secret"))     # make test data
;     dec = DataBuffer(bytes(c ^ 0x41 for c in bytes(enc)))    # decode: bytes() first, then xor
;     print(bytes(dec))                                        # b'secret'


; --- Notes
; - bytes(buf) is the door between DataBuffer and normal Python. Use it all the time.
; - buf[i] gives bytes (b'h'), but Python bytes[i] gives an int (104). Be careful.
; - A slice with a step is WRONG: buf[0:6:2] gives b'012345' (step ignored). Use bytes(buf)[0:6:2] -> b'024'.
; - Do not write into a DataBuffer with []. Use the bytearray way (Example 6).
; - str(buf) never fails: if it is not UTF-8, it uses latin-1. Example: b'\xff\xfeab' -> 'ÿþab'.












## debuginfo module (DebugInfo, DebugInfoParser, DebugFunctionInfo)
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Debug info = extra information the compiler can put in a binary (or in a separate file):
; real function names, function types, struct types, global variable names, local variable names.
; Formats: DWARF (Linux, in the ELF or in a separate .debug file), PDB (Windows, a separate .pdb file).
;
; Binja uses debug info as "ground truth": it trusts it more than its own guesses.
; So with debug info, the HLIL is faster, more correct, and has real names.
;
; The 3 classes:
;   DebugInfoParser    a reader for one format (DWARF, PDB, or your own). It reads a file and makes a DebugInfo.
;   DebugInfo          a box that holds the info: types, functions, data variables.
;                      It can hold info from MANY parsers. It is not tied to one binary.
;   DebugFunctionInfo  info about one function: address, names, type, local variables.
;
; The flow:
;   file --(DebugInfoParser.parse_debug_info)--> DebugInfo --(bv.apply_debug_info)--> your analysis
;
; Normally Binja does this BY ITSELF when you open a binary (settings analysis.debugInfo.internal / .external).
; You use this module to READ the info, to load a separate debug file, or to write your own parser.
;
; NOTE (from the source): DebugInfo.add_* functions only work inside a debug info parser.

; --- On the BinaryView
bv.debug_info                                     # the DebugInfo of this binary                           @binja @api @debuginfo  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/binaryview.py:10822>
bv.apply_debug_info(di)                           # set the DebugInfo AND use it in the analysis           @binja @api @debuginfo  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/binaryview.py:10835>
bv.debug_info = di                                # only set it, do not apply                              @binja @api @debuginfo

; --- DebugInfoParser
DebugInfoParser.list                              # all parsers                                            @binja @api @debuginfo  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/debuginfo.py:166>
DebugInfoParser.get_parsers_for_view(bv)          # parsers that can read this binary                      @binja @api @debuginfo
DebugInfoParser[name]                             # one parser by name                                     @binja @api @debuginfo
parser.name                                       # name                                                   @binja @api @debuginfo
parser.is_valid_for_view(view)                    # True if this parser can read this view                 @binja @api @debuginfo
parser.parse_debug_info(bv, debug_bv)             # read debug_bv, give a DebugInfo for bv                 @binja @api @debuginfo
DebugInfoParser.register(name, is_valid, parse)   # make your own parser                                   @binja @api @debuginfo

; --- DebugInfo (di)
di.parsers                                        # names of the parsers that gave info                    @binja @api @debuginfo  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/debuginfo.py:295>
di.functions                                      # all functions (DebugFunctionInfo)                      @binja @api @debuginfo
di.types                                          # all types: (name, Type)                                @binja @api @debuginfo
di.data_variables                                 # all globals: .address .type .name                      @binja @api @debuginfo
di.functions_from_parser(name)                    # same, but only from one parser                         @binja @api @debuginfo
di.types_from_parser(name)                        #                                                        @binja @api @debuginfo
di.data_variables_from_parser(name)               #                                                        @binja @api @debuginfo
di.get_types_by_name(name)                        # list of (parser_name, Type)                            @binja @api @debuginfo
di.get_data_variables_by_name(name)               # list of (parser_name, Type)                            @binja @api @debuginfo
di.get_data_variables_by_address(addr)            # list of (parser_name, Type)                            @binja @api @debuginfo
di.get_type_by_name(parser, name)                 # one Type, or None                                      @binja @api @debuginfo
di.remove_parser_info(parser)                     # remove everything from one parser                      @binja @api @debuginfo
di.add_type / add_function / add_data_variable    # add info (ONLY inside a parser)                        @binja @api @debuginfo

; --- DebugFunctionInfo (fi)
fi.address  fi.short_name  fi.full_name  fi.raw_name  fi.function_type  fi.platform  fi.components  fi.local_variables     @binja @api @debuginfo  <file:/opt/binaryninja/python/binaryninja/debuginfo.py:266>
;   short_name  "foo"
;   full_name   "ns::Class::foo(int)"
;   raw_name    "_ZN2ns5Class3fooEi"   (the mangled name)

; All examples below run in the Binja GUI Python console.
; bv = the binary you have open (the console gives you this variable).
; Use a binary WITH debug info (compile with: gcc -g test.c -o test), or nothing will print.


; --- Example 1: which parsers exist, and which can read my binary?
;
;     from binaryninja import DebugInfoParser
;     print("all:  ", [p.name for p in DebugInfoParser.list])
;     print("valid:", [p.name for p in DebugInfoParser.get_parsers_for_view(bv)])


; --- Example 2: what is in the debug info of my binary?
;
;     di = bv.debug_info
;     print("parsers:", list(di.parsers))
;     print("functions:", len(list(di.functions)))
;     print("types:    ", len(list(di.types)))
;     print("globals:  ", len(list(di.data_variables)))
;
; Note: functions / types / data_variables are GENERATORS. You can loop on them one time.
;       Use list(...) if you need them again.


; --- Example 3: list functions with their real types
;
;     di = bv.debug_info
;     for fi in di.functions:
;         addr = hex(fi.address) if fi.address else "no address"
;         print(addr, fi.full_name or fi.short_name, "|", fi.function_type)


; --- Example 4: list structs from the debug info
;
;     from binaryninja import StructureType
;     di = bv.debug_info
;     for name, t in di.types:
;         if isinstance(t, StructureType):
;             print(name, "size:", t.width)


; --- Example 5: list global variables
;
;     di = bv.debug_info
;     for v in di.data_variables:
;         print(hex(v.address), v.type, v.name)


; --- Example 6: find a type or a global by name
;
;     di = bv.debug_info
;     for parser_name, t in di.get_types_by_name("FILE"):
;         print(parser_name, t)
;     for parser_name, t in di.get_data_variables_by_name("stdout"):
;         print(parser_name, t)


; --- Example 7: load a SEPARATE debug file (stripped binary + .debug file)
; Linux distros ship stripped binaries. The debug info is in another file, for example:
;     /usr/lib/debug/.build-id/ab/cdef....debug
; You open the stripped binary in the GUI, then:
;
;     import binaryninja
;     from binaryninja import DebugInfoParser
;     debug_bv = binaryninja.load("/path/to/program.debug", update_analysis=False)
;     for parser in DebugInfoParser.get_parsers_for_view(debug_bv):
;         di = parser.parse_debug_info(bv, debug_bv)        # bv = stripped binary, debug_bv = the debug file
;         if di is not None:
;             bv.apply_debug_info(di)
;             print("applied", parser.name)
;
; After this, functions get real names and types. Wait for the analysis to finish.
; (You can also set the setting analysis.debugInfo.external to the debug file path, before Binja opens the binary.)


; --- Example 8: find functions that debug info knows, but Binja did not find
;
;     di = bv.debug_info
;     for fi in di.functions:
;         if fi.address and bv.get_function_at(fi.address) is None:
;             print("missing:", hex(fi.address), fi.short_name)


; --- Example 9: your own parser -> read a simple symbol map file
; Some tools give you a text file like:   0x401136 main   (one function per line).
; This parser reads it and gives names to the functions.
;
;     import os
;     import binaryninja as bn
;     from binaryninja import DebugInfoParser, DebugFunctionInfo
;
;     MAP_FILE = "/tmp/symbols.txt"                         # your file: "0x401136 main" per line
;
;     def is_valid(view):
;         return os.path.exists(MAP_FILE)                   # this parser works only if the file exists
;
;     def parse_info(debug_info, view, debug_file, progress):
;         for line in open(MAP_FILE):
;             parts = line.split()
;             if len(parts) != 2:
;                 continue
;             addr, name = int(parts[0], 16), parts[1]
;             debug_info.add_function(DebugFunctionInfo(address=addr, short_name=name, full_name=name, raw_name=name))
;         return True
;
;     DebugInfoParser.register("symbol_map", is_valid, parse_info)
;
;     parser = DebugInfoParser["symbol_map"]
;     di = parser.parse_debug_info(bv, bv)                  # one file only -> give bv two times
;     bv.apply_debug_info(di)
;
; Note: when the debug info is in the SAME file, give bv two times. (The source says this.)


; --- Example 10: give a function with a full type
; Inside parse_info (Example 9), you can also give the function type. Binja then uses it in HLIL.
;
;     from binaryninja import DebugFunctionInfo, Type
;     ftype = Type.function(Type.int(4), [Type.int(4), Type.pointer(view.arch, Type.char())])
;     debug_info.add_function(DebugFunctionInfo(address=0x401136, short_name="main", function_type=ftype))
;
; Rule: give only what you KNOW. Binja finds the rest by itself.


; --- Notes
; - di.functions / di.types / di.data_variables are generators: loop one time, or wrap in list(...).
; - A DebugFunctionInfo with NO address does not make a function. But you can still read it from bv.debug_info.
; - add_type has a "components" argument, but the source says it is not used now.
; - bv.debug_info = di only stores it. bv.apply_debug_info(di) stores it AND changes the analysis.
; - I did not run these examples.
